This is because each component instance has its own `isExpanded` variable. This independence is another powerful feature of component-based design.

## Part 3: Introduction to derived values

Now that you understand the basics of state and event handlers, let's build something that introduces a new concept: values that automatically calculate themselves from other values.

We're going to build an in-class survey. I'm going to ask how many of you subscribe to each of the major streaming services. You'll click buttons to record the votes, and the component will tally everything up in real time.

This is exactly the kind of interactive element that news organizations build for reader engagement. You've probably seen polls embedded in stories asking readers to weigh in on a topic. "Do you think the city should raise the minimum wage?" "Should the Olympics add esports?" These tools are simple to build, and they demonstrate a powerful Svelte concept.

### Starting with one service

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/StreamingSurvey.svelte`. Let's start small, with just one streaming service:

```svelte
<script>
  let netflixYes = $state(0);
  let netflixNo = $state(0);
</script>

<div class="survey">
  <h3>Streaming Survey</h3>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Netflix</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => netflixYes++}>
        Yes ({netflixYes})
      </button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => netflixNo++}>
        No ({netflixNo})
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

This should feel familiar from Part 1. We have two `$state` variables, one for yes votes and one for no votes. Each button increments its counter when clicked. The current count is displayed right on the button.

Copy and paste the styles I've provided below. We won't spend much time on the CSS today—our focus is on the JavaScript. But the green and red button colors help make the yes/no distinction clear at a glance.

```svelte
<style>
  .survey {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .service {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.75rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
  }

  .name {
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .buttons {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  button {
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    border: none;
    font-weight: bold;
    cursor: pointer;
    color: white;
  }

  .yes-btn {
    background: #2e7d32;
  }

  .no-btn {
    background: #c62828;
  }
</style>
```

Save the file, then open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and import it:

```js
import StreamingSurvey from '$lib/components/StreamingSurvey.svelte';
```

Place it in your page:

```svelte
<StreamingSurvey />
```

Check your browser. You should see Netflix with yes and no buttons. Click them and watch the counts update. It works, but it's just one service. Let's expand.

### Introduction to $derived()

Before we add more services, let's introduce a new concept. What if we want to know the total number of votes for Netflix? We could write `{netflixYes + netflixNo}` in our markup, and it would work. But Svelte offers a better way: the `$derived()` rune.

```svelte
<script>
  let netflixYes = $state(0);
  let netflixNo = $state(0);
  let netflixTotal = $derived(netflixYes + netflixNo);
</script>
```

The `$derived()` rune creates a value that automatically recalculates whenever the values it depends on change. When `netflixYes` or `netflixNo` changes, `netflixTotal` automatically updates. You never have to manually recalculate it. Svelte handles the math for you.

We can derive more than just a sum. How about the percentage of yes votes?

```svelte
<script>
  let netflixYes = $state(0);
  let netflixNo = $state(0);
  let netflixTotal = $derived(netflixYes + netflixNo);
  let netflixPercent = $derived(
    netflixTotal > 0 ? Math.round((netflixYes / netflixTotal) * 100) : 0
  );
</script>
```

Notice the `netflixTotal > 0 ?` check. This prevents a division-by-zero error when no votes have been cast. If there are no votes, we just show 0%. Otherwise, we calculate the percentage and round it to a whole number with `Math.round()`.

Let's add this to our display. Update the markup to show the total and percentage below the buttons:

```svelte
<div class="service">
  <span class="name">Netflix</span>
  <div class="buttons">
    <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => netflixYes++}>
      Yes ({netflixYes})
    </button>
    <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => netflixNo++}>
      No ({netflixNo})
    </button>
  </div>
  <span class="percent">{netflixPercent}%</span>
</div>
```

Add a style for the percentage to your `<style>` block:

```css
.percent {
  font-weight: bold;
  min-width: 50px;
  text-align: right;
}
```

Save and test. As you click yes and no, the percentage updates instantly. This is `$derived()` in action—you change the inputs, and every value that depends on them recalculates automatically.

### Scaling up to multiple services

Now let's add the rest of our streaming services. Update the script block with state and derived values for each one:

```svelte
<script>
  // Netflix
  let netflixYes = $state(0);
  let netflixNo = $state(0);
  let netflixTotal = $derived(netflixYes + netflixNo);
  let netflixPercent = $derived(
    netflixTotal > 0 ? Math.round((netflixYes / netflixTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Hulu
  let huluYes = $state(0);
  let huluNo = $state(0);
  let huluTotal = $derived(huluYes + huluNo);
  let huluPercent = $derived(
    huluTotal > 0 ? Math.round((huluYes / huluTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Disney+
  let disneyYes = $state(0);
  let disneyNo = $state(0);
  let disneyTotal = $derived(disneyYes + disneyNo);
  let disneyPercent = $derived(
    disneyTotal > 0 ? Math.round((disneyYes / disneyTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Max
  let maxYes = $state(0);
  let maxNo = $state(0);
  let maxTotal = $derived(maxYes + maxNo);
  let maxPercent = $derived(
    maxTotal > 0 ? Math.round((maxYes / maxTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Apple TV+
  let appleYes = $state(0);
  let appleNo = $state(0);
  let appleTotal = $derived(appleYes + appleNo);
  let applePercent = $derived(
    appleTotal > 0 ? Math.round((appleYes / appleTotal) * 100) : 0
  );

  // Totals across all services
  let totalYes = $derived(netflixYes + huluYes + disneyYes + maxYes + appleYes);
  let totalNo = $derived(netflixNo + huluNo + disneyNo + maxNo + appleNo);
  let totalVotes = $derived(totalYes + totalNo);
  let overallPercent = $derived(
    totalVotes > 0 ? Math.round((totalYes / totalVotes) * 100) : 0
  );
</script>
```

Yes, that's a lot of variables. You might be thinking there has to be a better way to organize this, and you'd be right. Later in the course, when we work with data structures like arrays and objects, we'll learn how to handle repetitive patterns more elegantly. For now, the repetition is actually useful, because it makes the pattern crystal clear: state in, derived values out.

Look at those last four lines. `totalYes` and `totalNo` derive from all five services. `totalVotes` derives from `totalYes` and `totalNo`. And `overallPercent` derives from `totalYes` and `totalVotes`. This is a chain of derived values—each one building on the ones before it. Svelte keeps every link in the chain up to date automatically.

Now update the markup with all five services and a summary footer:

```svelte
<div class="survey">
  <h3>📺 Do you subscribe to...</h3>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Netflix</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => netflixYes++}>Yes ({netflixYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => netflixNo++}>No ({netflixNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{netflixPercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Hulu</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => huluYes++}>Yes ({huluYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => huluNo++}>No ({huluNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{huluPercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Disney+</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => disneyYes++}>Yes ({disneyYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => disneyNo++}>No ({disneyNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{disneyPercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Max</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => maxYes++}>Yes ({maxYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => maxNo++}>No ({maxNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{maxPercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="service">
    <span class="name">Apple TV+</span>
    <div class="buttons">
      <button class="yes-btn" onclick={() => appleYes++}>Yes ({appleYes})</button>
      <button class="no-btn" onclick={() => appleNo++}>No ({appleNo})</button>
    </div>
    <span class="percent">{applePercent}%</span>
  </div>

  <div class="summary">
    <div class="summary-stat">
      <span class="summary-number">{totalVotes}</span>
      <span class="summary-label">Total Votes</span>
    </div>
    <div class="summary-stat">
      <span class="summary-number">{totalYes}</span>
      <span class="summary-label">Subscriptions</span>
    </div>
    <div class="summary-stat">
      <span class="summary-number">{overallPercent}%</span>
      <span class="summary-label">Subscribe Rate</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

Add the summary styles to your `<style>` block:

```css
.summary {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-around;
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding-top: 1rem;
  border-top: 2px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
}

.summary-stat {
  text-align: center;
}

.summary-number {
  display: block;
  font-size: 2rem;
  font-weight: bold;
}

.summary-label {
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}
```

Save and check your browser. You should see all five streaming services with yes and no buttons, individual percentages, and a summary row at the bottom that tallies everything up.

Let's try it. I'll poll the room. Raise your hand if you subscribe to Netflix. Count the hands, click yes that many times. Now the rest of you, click no. Move on to Hulu. Disney+. Max. Apple TV+.

Watch the summary row as we go. The total votes climb. The subscription count rises. The overall percentage adjusts. Every single number on the page is either a `$state` that we're clicking to change, or a `$derived` value that recalculates automatically. Nothing is stale. Nothing is out of sync.

### Why use $derived()?

You might wonder why we need `$derived()` when we could just write `{netflixYes + netflixNo}` directly in the template. There are a few reasons:

1. **Organization**: Keeping calculations in the script block makes your template cleaner and your logic easier to find.

2. **Reuse**: If you need to use the total in multiple places, you only define it once.

3. **Complexity**: For more complex calculations, `$derived()` keeps things readable. Imagine a formula like `$derived(netflixTotal > 0 ? Math.round((netflixYes / netflixTotal) * 100) : 0)` for a percentage—that would clutter your template.

4. **Chaining**: Derived values can depend on other derived values. Our `overallPercent` depends on `totalYes` and `totalVotes`, which themselves depend on the individual service counts. Svelte sorts out the dependency chain for you.

<Aside title="The rising cost of streaming">
  <p>If you're curious about how much all these subscriptions add up to, the numbers are staggering. As of early 2026, Netflix's ad-free plan costs $17.99 a month. Disney+ is $11.99 with ads. Max is $10.99. Apple TV+ is $12.99. Hulu is $9.99. That's over $63 a month just for five services—and it doesn't include the ad-free tiers, which can push the total well past $90. According to a 2025 survey by Reviews.org, U.S. consumers spent an average of $278 per month to stay connected to their chosen streaming platforms. News organizations cover this "streamflation" beat regularly, often with exactly the kind of interactive calculators we're learning to build.</p>
</Aside>

## Part 4: Introduction to two-way binding

For our final component, we're going to build something more ambitious: a tip calculator that responds to form inputs. This will introduce one of Svelte's most powerful features: two-way data binding with `bind:value`.

News organizations love calculators. They let readers personalize a story by entering their own information. "How much would you save under the new tax plan?" "What's your risk level based on these factors?" "How does your commute compare to the city average?"

These tools increase engagement because they make the story about the reader. And with Svelte, they're surprisingly easy to build.

### Understanding two-way binding

So far, we've seen one-way interactions: the user clicks a button, and something changes. But form inputs work differently. A text field or slider needs to both display a value and accept changes to that value.

Svelte handles this with `bind:value`, which creates a two-way connection between an input element and a state variable. When the variable changes, the input updates. When the user types or drags, the variable updates. Everything stays in sync automatically.

Here's a simple example:

```svelte
<script>
  let name = $state('');
</script>

<input type="text" bind:value={name}>
<p>Hello, {name}!</p>
```

As the user types, the paragraph instantly updates to greet them by name. No event handlers needed—Svelte handles the connection for you.

### Building the calculator step by step

Let's build our tip calculator in stages, adding features as we go.

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/TipCalculator.svelte`. Start with the most basic version:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tip = $derived(billAmount * 0.20);
</script>

<div class="calculator">
  <h3>Tip Calculator</h3>

  <div class="input-group">
    <label for="bill">Bill Amount</label>
    <div class="input-wrapper">
      <span class="currency">$</span>
      <input
        type="number"
        id="bill"
        bind:value={billAmount}
        min="0"
        step="0.01"
      >
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="result">
    <span class="label">20% Tip:</span>
    <span class="value">${tip.toFixed(2)}</span>
  </div>
</div>
```

The `bind:value={billAmount}` creates our two-way binding. The input displays the current value of `billAmount`, and typing a new number updates the variable.

The `tip` variable uses `$derived()` to calculate 20% of the bill. Notice the `.toFixed(2)` at the end—this formats the number to always show two decimal places, which is what we expect for currency.

Add some basic styling. Again, just copy and paste this—we'll focus more on CSS in later weeks:

```svelte
<style>
  .calculator {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
    margin: 2rem 0;
    max-width: 400px;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .input-group {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

