</style>
```

Import it into your page and try it out:

```js
import TipCalculator from '$lib/components/TipCalculator.svelte';
```

```svelte
<TipCalculator />
```

Type a bill amount and watch the tip calculate instantly. But 20% isn't always the right tip. Let's add a way to customize the percentage.

### Adding a custom tip percentage

Update your script to include a tip percentage variable:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tipPercent = $state(18);
  let tip = $derived(billAmount * (tipPercent / 100));
</script>
```

Now our `tip` calculation uses the `tipPercent` variable instead of a hardcoded 0.20. We start at 18% because that's a common default.

Add a range slider to let users adjust the percentage. Insert this after the bill amount input:

```svelte
<div class="input-group">
  <label for="percent">Tip: {tipPercent}%</label>
  <input
    type="range"
    id="percent"
    bind:value={tipPercent}
    min="10"
    max="30"
    step="1"
  >
</div>
```

The range input creates a slider. `bind:value={tipPercent}` connects it to our state. As the user drags, `tipPercent` updates, which triggers `tip` to recalculate, which updates the display. The label shows the current percentage using `{tipPercent}%`.

Update your result display to show the dynamic percentage:

```svelte
<div class="result">
  <span class="label">{tipPercent}% Tip:</span>
  <span class="value">${tip.toFixed(2)}</span>
</div>
```

Add styling for the slider:

```css
input[type="range"] {
  width: 100%;
}
```

### Adding the total

Let's show the total bill including tip:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tipPercent = $state(18);
  let tip = $derived(billAmount * (tipPercent / 100));
  let total = $derived(billAmount + tip);
</script>
```

Add another result display:

```svelte
<div class="results">
  <div class="result">
    <span class="label">{tipPercent}% Tip:</span>
    <span class="value">${tip.toFixed(2)}</span>
  </div>
  <div class="result total">
    <span class="label">Total:</span>
    <span class="value">${total.toFixed(2)}</span>
  </div>
</div>
```

Update the styles to handle multiple results:

```css
.results {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  margin-top: 1rem;
}

.result.total {
  background: var(--color-dark-gray);
}
```

### Splitting the bill

For the final feature, let's add the ability to split the bill among multiple people:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tipPercent = $state(18);
  let numPeople = $state(1);

  let tip = $derived(billAmount * (tipPercent / 100));
  let total = $derived(billAmount + tip);
  let perPerson = $derived(total / numPeople);
</script>
```

Add an input for the number of people, after the tip slider:

```svelte
<div class="input-group">
  <label>Split between</label>
  <div class="people-input">
    <button onclick={() => numPeople = Math.max(1, numPeople - 1)}>−</button>
    <span class="people-count">{numPeople}</span>
    <button onclick={() => numPeople++}>+</button>
  </div>
</div>
```

Notice we use `Math.max(1, numPeople - 1)` for the minus button. This ensures we never go below 1 person—you can't split a bill zero ways!

Add the per-person result:

```svelte
{#if numPeople > 1}
  <div class="result per-person">
    <span class="label">Per Person:</span>
    <span class="value">${perPerson.toFixed(2)}</span>
  </div>
{/if}
```

The `{#if numPeople > 1}` only shows this line when splitting among multiple people. No need to show "per person" when there's only one person.

Here's the styling for the people input and the per-person result:

```css
.people-input {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1rem;
}

.people-input button {
  width: 40px;
  height: 40px;
  border: none;
  background: var(--color-accent);
  color: white;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.people-count {
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  font-weight: bold;
}

.result.per-person {
  background: #1565c0;
}
```

### The complete component

Here's the final `TipCalculator.svelte` with all features:

```svelte
<script>
  let billAmount = $state(0);
  let tipPercent = $state(18);
  let numPeople = $state(1);

  let tip = $derived(billAmount * (tipPercent / 100));
  let total = $derived(billAmount + tip);
  let perPerson = $derived(total / numPeople);
</script>

<div class="calculator">
  <h3>Tip Calculator</h3>

  <div class="input-group">
    <label for="bill">Bill Amount</label>
    <div class="input-wrapper">
      <span class="currency">$</span>
      <input
        type="number"
        id="bill"
        bind:value={billAmount}
        min="0"
        step="0.01"
      >
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="input-group">
    <label for="percent">Tip: {tipPercent}%</label>
    <input
      type="range"
      id="percent"
      bind:value={tipPercent}
      min="10"
      max="30"
      step="1"
    >
  </div>

  <div class="input-group">
    <label>Split between</label>
    <div class="people-input">
      <button onclick={() => numPeople = Math.max(1, numPeople - 1)}>−</button>
      <span class="people-count">{numPeople}</span>
      <button onclick={() => numPeople++}>+</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="results">
    <div class="result">
      <span class="label">{tipPercent}% Tip:</span>
      <span class="value">${tip.toFixed(2)}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="result total">
      <span class="label">Total:</span>
      <span class="value">${total.toFixed(2)}</span>
    </div>
    {#if numPeople > 1}
      <div class="result per-person">
        <span class="label">Per Person:</span>
        <span class="value">${perPerson.toFixed(2)}</span>
      </div>
    {/if}
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .calculator {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--color-light-gray);
    margin: 2rem 0;
    max-width: 400px;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .input-group {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  label {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .input-wrapper {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    border: 2px solid #ddd;
  }

  .currency {
    padding: 0.75rem;
    background: #eee;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  input[type="number"] {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    border: none;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    width: 100%;
  }

  input[type="range"] {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .people-input {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .people-input button {
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;
    border: none;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: white;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .people-count {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .results {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .result {
    padding: 1rem;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: white;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .result .label {
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .result .value {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .result.total {
    background: var(--color-dark-gray);
  }

  .result.per-person {
    background: #1565c0;
  }
</style>
```

## Homework

### Task 1: Build three interactive components

Create a new repository from our class template and build three interactive components from scratch. Pick from this list or invent your own:

- **Time until election countdown**: Accept a date input and display days, hours, minutes until that date. Update automatically (hint: you'll need `setInterval`).

- **Rent split calculator**: Enter total rent and number of roommates. Show what each person owes. Bonus: allow unequal splits with percentage inputs.

- **Reading time estimator**: Paste text into a textarea. Calculate and display estimated reading time based on word count (assume 200 words per minute).

- **Quiz question**: Display a multiple choice question. When the user selects an answer, show whether they're right or wrong with an explanation.

- **Unit converter**: Convert between miles and kilometers, Fahrenheit and Celsius, or dollars and euros. Let users type in either field and see the other update.

Deploy your page to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Pick your favorite interactive component to show the class. Be ready to explain:

- What state does your component track?
- What user actions trigger state changes?
- How does the display update in response?
- If you used AI to help, what worked and what didn't?

We'll spend the first part of next class sharing what you built.
